-- title:
--   Theorem 6.13 — on the good event $|\sum_{\tau\le t}\rho_\tau|\le 4\sum_{\tau\le t}\max\{\psi_{\tau-1}(A_{\tau,0}),\psi_{\tau-1}(B_{\tau,0})\}$
-- statement:
--   Assume the valuation law $\mathcal V$ satisfies the standing assumptions (support in $[0,1]^2$, $\mathbb P(V^{\mathrm{buy}}=1)=\mathbb P(V^{\mathrm{sell}}=0)=0$), and run Algorithm 4 in the probability model of Sec. 3 on finite grids $\mathcal B^\circ,\mathcal A^\circ\subset[0,1]$ containing a pair $b<a$, with nonnegative radii $\psi_t$ that are $\mathcal F_t$-measurable, and $\psi_0=1$. Let $\rho_\tau=\mathbb E[\mathbb I(V^{\mathrm{sell}}_\tau\le B_\tau)-\mathbb I(V^{\mathrm{buy}}_\tau\ge A_\tau)\mid\mathcal F_{\tau-1}]$. Then, on the good event (16) up to $T$, for every $t\in\{1,\dots,T\}$,
--   $$
--   \Big|\sum_{\tau=1}^t\rho_\tau\Big|\le4\sum_{\tau=1}^t\max\{\psi_{\tau-1}(A_{\tau,0}),\psi_{\tau-1}(B_{\tau,0})\},
--   $$
--   where $(B_{\tau,0},A_{\tau,0})$ is the candidate pair of round $\tau$. The randomized correction keeps the expected drift of the inventory within the width of the confidence intervals.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: arbitrary discretizations B°, A° of [0, 1] (Sec. 6.2) and radius functions of Notation 6.9 → finite grids in [0, 1] that contain a pair b < a, and radii that are nonnegative and F_t-measurable (adapted); reason: the model requires 0 ≤ B_t < A_t ≤ 1 (p. 14), which needs a grid pair b < a (see Proposition 6.11); every confidence radius is nonnegative, and Notation 6.9 says the radius of time t depends only on quantities generated by the end of round t
--   - generalization: radii ψ_t such that (16) holds with probability at least 1 − δ (Notation 6.9) → any radii with the stated measurability/sign conditions; reason: the conclusion is asserted on the good event, so the probability of (16) is never used; the paper's radii are instances
--   - encoding: under the good event → for P-almost every outcome of the good event; reason: ρ_τ and E[r_τ | F_{τ−1}] are conditional expectations, defined up to null sets
--   - reading: F_t contains all valuations and algorithmic random draws through round t (pp. 42, 47) → F_t = σ(W_1, …, W_t) with W_s = ((V^sell_s, V^buy_s), U_s); F_0 trivial; reason: this is the σ-algebra the sentence describes
--   - encoding: T rounds with (V^sell_t, V^buy_t) i.i.d. from V and the Bernoulli draws X ∼ Bern(p), Y ∼ Bern(p) of Algorithm 4 → measurable sequences indexed by ℕ whose round vectors ((V^sell_t, V^buy_t), U_t) are mutually independent with law V ⊗ Unif[0,1]; a draw Bern(p) is 1{U_t < p}; index 0 and indices beyond T are unused; reason: given the past, 1{U_t < p} has law Bern(p); at most one draw is made per round; every event involves rounds 1, …, T only
--   - encoding: Algorithm 5 has no return statement after its second loop (Line 26) → it then returns the outer pair (b_0, a_{M−1}); reason: under this statement's hypotheses (a grid pair b < a, nonnegative radii) the loop always returns (Proposition 6.11), so the fallback is never used
--   - reading: Line 23 of Algorithm 4 updates F̂_t, Ŝ_t on the active sets only → F̂_t(b) = (1/t) Σ_{τ≤t} χ^sell_τ(b) and Ŝ_t(a) = (1/t) Σ_{τ≤t} χ^buy_τ(a) for every price, with F̂_0 = Ŝ_0 = 0; reason: Algorithm 5 evaluates the bounds only on the active sets, which are nested, so the values the algorithm uses are the same
--   - reading: S(a) = E[I(V^buy ≥ a)] = 1 − F_buy(a) (p. 16) → S(a) = P(V^buy ≥ a); reason: the two expressions differ exactly at atoms of V^buy; the trade probability E[I(V^buy ≥ a)] is what the model and every proof use (p. 59 also writes S(a) = P(−V^buy ≤ −a))
-- source:
--   Maran, Restelli, Stradi, No-Regret Revenue Maximization in Sequential Market Making Under Inventory Constraints, arXiv:2610.24562v1, Theorem 6.13, p. 43

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_InvMarketMaking_LOB_IsValuationLaw
import Definitions.Def_InvMarketMaking_LOB_IsRoundModel
import Definitions.Def_InvMarketMaking_LOB_lobRound
import Definitions.Def_InvMarketMaking_LOB_goodEvent

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace InvMarketMaking.LOB

/-- **Theorem 6.13** (Maran, Restelli, Stradi, arXiv:2610.24562v1, p. 43). Under the good event (16),
`|Σ_{τ=1}^t ρ_τ| ≤ 4 Σ_{τ=1}^t max{ψ_{τ−1}(A_{τ,0}), ψ_{τ−1}(B_{τ,0})}`, where
`ρ_τ = E[I(V^sell_τ ≤ B_τ) − I(V^buy_τ ≥ A_τ) | ℱ_{τ−1}]` and `ψ_0 ≡ 1`.

**Formalization Note** Stated for Algorithm 4 with grids in `[0,1]` containing a pair `b < a`, and
nonnegative radii adapted to `(ℱ_t)` (Notation 6.9). The radii are evaluated at the candidate
prices `(B_{τ,0}, A_{τ,0})` of Algorithm 5. The bound holds for every `t ∈ [T]` at almost every
outcome of the good event (the conditional expectation is defined up to null sets). -/
theorem theorem_6_13 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    (V : Measure (ℝ × ℝ)) [IsProbabilityMeasure V] (hV : IsValuationLaw V)
    (M : Market Ω) (hM : IsRoundModel P V M) (C : LOBConfig Ω)
    (hB : ↑C.Bg ⊆ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) (hA : ↑C.Ag ⊆ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1)
    (hfeas : ∃ b ∈ C.Bg, ∃ a ∈ C.Ag, b < a)
    (hψF : ∀ t x ω, 0 ≤ C.ψF t x ω) (hψS : ∀ t x ω, 0 ≤ C.ψS t x ω)
    (hψFm : ∀ t x, Measurable[roundSigma M t] (C.ψF t x))
    (hψSm : ∀ t x, Measurable[roundSigma M t] (C.ψS t x)) (T : ℕ) :
    ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ω ∈ goodEvent V C M T → ∀ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T,
      |∑ τ ∈ Finset.Icc 1 t, (P[invIncrement C M τ | roundSigma M (τ - 1)]) ω| ≤
        4 * ∑ τ ∈ Finset.Icc 1 t,
          max (radS C (τ - 1) (lobRound C M τ ω).ask0 ω)
            (radF C (τ - 1) (lobRound C M τ ω).bid0 ω) := by sorry

end InvMarketMaking.LOB
